<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=sundial">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sundial</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A sundial tells the time of day from the apparent position of the Sun, usually by the shadow that a gnomon casts on a dial marked with hour-lines. The earliest sundials known from the archaeological record are shadow clocks of about 1500 BCE from ancient Egyptian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">Babylonian astronomy</a>. By 240 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eratosthenes">Eratosthenes</a> had used an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisk</a> and a water well to estimate the circumference of the world, and the Roman writer Vitruvius listed the dials known in his day in De architectura. Canonical sundials marked the liturgical hours of religious orders from the 7th to the 14th centuries, and Giovanni Padovani published a treatise on making sundials in 1570.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sundial.jpg" alt="Sundial" width="330" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wilson44691 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MootHallSundial.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It recovered under later rulers and in 263 BCE was one of the first Sicilian cities taken by Rome, which carried off its <b>sundial</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/catana">Catana</a></li></ul></section>
